  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061z)\\,\\text{d}y-y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0)\\,\\text{d}x+(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\,\\text{d}y-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+\u0394x,0+\u0394y,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\u0394z),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\u0394f\u22480\\,\u0394x+1\\,\u0394y+0\\,\u0394z.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394x & =0-1=-1, \\\\ \u0394y & =1-0=1 \\\\ \u0394z & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394f & \u22480\\,\u0394x+1\\,\u0394y+0\\,\u0394z \\\\  & \u22480\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (1)+0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & \u22481.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $1.$",
